const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "pk:0": [
    "In Pakistan, fables do not sleep in one place. They set out from Karachi, beside the sea, then climb the Indus valley to the cold mountains. In the courtyard, the grandfather Farid sits his grandchildren down and says: ‘We do not have just one book. We have a caravan.’ In that caravan travel Mughal tales and Sufi stories. There is also the Punjabi qissa, which is sung, and the Sindhi tale of the riverbank. Whoever arrives brings a story. Whoever stays offers tea. On that night, the whole country fits between the port and the mountains.",
    "Farid begins with the wise man who makes people laugh. He is Mulla Nasruddin, known from the Punjab to Sindh. One night, Nasruddin was looking for a key under a lamp, on his knees in the dust. A neighbour asked: ‘Did you lose the key here?’ Nasruddin shook his head: ‘No. I lost it in the dark, right at the back of the yard.’ The neighbour was surprised: ‘So why are you looking here?’ The wise man smiled: ‘Because here there is light.’ The children laugh, and Farid explains that laughter teaches. Sometimes we only look where it is easy, and the true key stays in the shadow.",
    "After the laughter, Farid asks for silence. He tells the story of the reed pipe, the Sufi tale that Rumi left. The Punjab and Sindh still feel it. The reed was cut from the reed bed where it grew with its sisters. People made a flute from it. Since that day the pipe weeps, not from spite, but because it remembers the separation. A small boy asks: ‘Why is it sad, if the music is beautiful?’ Farid answers: ‘Because the heart was also cut from a place, and it looks for the way back.’ The sweet sound and the weeping are the same voice.",
    "The next morning, Farid takes his grandson to the road that leaves Karachi towards the mountains. A traveller goes past. He speaks Urdu in the market, Punjabi in the song, and Sindhi on the boat. He switches to Pashto as the mountains come closer. The boy hears all four and says: ‘They are different languages.’ The grandfather answers: ‘Yes, and the message is the same.’ In one night at an inn, the traveller tells both the key of Nasruddin and the weeping of the pipe. Nobody asks him to choose just one story. Hospitality in this land is also hospitality of stories.",
    "The boy keeps two images for life. One is the wise man laughing under the lamp. The other is the reed weeping a beautiful music. Without the Sufi, says Farid, the heart dries up like a field without a river. Without the qissa, the village falls silent and the night is empty. With both, Pakistan, with its sea and its peaks like K2, fits into one story told slowly. If you hear a laugh and a lament on the same night, do not choose. Both teach. The wise man laughs. The reed weeps. The country listens.",
  ],
  "pk:1": [
    "In the north of Pakistan, between very high peaks, the Hunza valley opens out. Apricots ripen like small gold, the water is clear, and the air seems cleaner than in the cities. Hunzakut children hear of the peri, mountain fairies who cross ice bridges and dislike noise. They also hear about a king, long ago, who hid a garden. He did it so that the hurry of the world would not spoil it. Anyone coming from Gilgit looks at the valley and thinks the garden may not be hidden at all. Perhaps it is the valley itself.",
    "They say Marco Polo passed by these mountains, when the world was still measured in caravan steps. He did not stop to count apricots, but the old road remembers that Hunza was never a forgotten corner. The people of the valley are the Burusho. They speak a language that looks nothing like their neighbours’ languages. That is also a closed garden: sounds that grew only here. A grandfather explains to his granddaughter that an isolated language is like a rare tree. If nobody hears it, the garden loses a colour, even if the peaks stay where they are.",
    "There is another story, repeated in the houses. It says the hunzakut people come from soldiers of Alexander, who came from Macedonia many centuries ago. It is a legend of pride and of a long road. Science argues, measures and compares, and does not shut the door with an easy yes or an angry no. So the granddaughter learns to say her doubt aloud, without shame. ‘It may be a tale, it may be memory, and it may be both mixed together,’ says the grandfather. Inventing certainty, here, would be less honest than offering tea to whoever asks.",
    "The legend also says that in Hunza people live for very many years. It is as if time walked more slowly among the apricots. The number is often exaggerated. We do not need to pretend that everyone reaches a hundred with a postcard smile. Under the exaggeration there is a simple truth. There is cold air, clean water, work on the land, and a life without too much hurry. In the granddaughter’s story, a peri sits in the shade with a white-haired grandfather and promises no miracles. She only asks that the spring is not made dirty. The peak alone is wonder enough.",
    "If the new road comes into the valley without respect, it eats the garden the king wanted to keep. It cuts the shade, makes noise on the peri’s bridge, and treats the water as if it were endless. With respect, the apricot, the mountain fairy and the grandfather who tells stories slowly fit in the same shade. The child of Gilgit-Baltistan does not need to invent another miracle. They need not to spoil this one. Hunza teaches that true long life begins by letting the valley breathe. The hidden garden stays in view, if you can look without taking it all.",
  ],
  "pk:2": [
    "On the banks of the Chenab river, in the Punjab, lived a girl called Sohni. On the other side lived Mahiwal, a young man who kept buffaloes and lit a small fire when night fell. The two met in the shallow water and talked from far off. Each heart crossed the river before their feet did. Their families did not want this love. They said the world already had marked paths, and that a river in the middle was answer enough. Sohni and Mahiwal did not argue in shouts. They arranged a meeting that only the night would see.",
    "Sohni did not swim like the ferrymen. So she carried a large jar of well-fired clay, light in the water. She held on to it like someone holding a promise. Every night she crossed the Chenab slowly, until she reached Mahiwal’s fire. He waited with a dry cloth and a short story. He never asked her to stay when the sky began to brighten. She went back before the houses woke. The Punjabi qissa is born from this going and coming. Love, here, was not a palace. It was a firm jar and a wide river.",
    "In Sohni’s house there was a sister-in-law with a heart tight with envy. She saw the girl leave, saw her come back with shining eyes, and could not bear that quiet joy. One afternoon, while nobody was looking, she swapped the fired jar. She put in another one, the same in shape but raw, not yet baked. Raw clay looks strong on a table. In water, it falls apart. The sister-in-law did not shout about the trick she had played. She put the false jar in place of the true one. Then she went to sit down as if the night were like any other.",
    "Sohni stepped into the Chenab with the jar in her arms. Halfway across, the clay softened, opened in cracks and lost its shape. She called Mahiwal, without anger, just with his name. He heard, ran from the bank and threw himself into the water to reach her. There was no fight and no monster. There was a river that does not forgive a trick. There were two people who did not let go of each other. The Chenab closed over them, dark and wide. The fire stayed alone on the bank, going out. The true jar stayed hidden at home, useless and too late.",
    "The Punjab tells this qissa with a tight heart, and the ending is not sweetened to make it comfortable. Pakistan and India share the same pair, the same bank, the same Chenab. The jar had to be well baked. So did the love. Only one of the two was ready, and envy did the rest. Children are told the truth in a low voice. The cruel trick is no joke. The river does not give back what a lie has pushed. If you love someone, do not swap their ground for something that falls apart.",
  ],
  "pk:3": [
    "People often speak of the swamp tiger, the one that swims among mangrove roots. That tiger rules the Sundarbans, more on the Bangladesh side than in Pakistan. Here, in the Indus valley, other animals take the story: the leopard of the high rocks and the river crocodile. In Sindh, the tale children hear first is not about a loose tooth. It is about Udero Lal, a saint loved by Hindus and Muslims, who walks on the water to calm it. The ancient Indus asks for a protector who shuts the door to nobody.",
    "Udero Lal does not choose one temple only. When the water is angry, Hindus and Muslims say his name on the same bank. They say the Indus crocodile sometimes asks for an offering, as if the river were charging a toll. The saint does not reply with fear or with cruelty. He teaches a rule. Take no more from the river than the pot needs, and do not dirty the spring. Do not treat your neighbour of another faith as an enemy of the water. Without the rule, the crocodile is only teeth. With it, even the tooth learns the limit.",
    "In a year of floods, a small boat was caught between two currents, near a Sindhi village. In it were a Muslim fisherman and the Hindu neighbour who had helped him push off the boat. The children on the bank called Udero Lal, not one by one, but together. A low light ran along the water, like a narrow path. The crocodile rose slowly, saw the light and did not move towards the boat. It is not a scare to make you shout. It is a warning that stops. In the story, the saint put his hand on the current and it obeyed.",
    "The boat touched the good mud of the bank. Nobody celebrated with pride. They shared bread and dried fish, Hindu and Muslim, on the same cloth. The fisherman said: ‘The river could have taken us.’ The neighbour answered: ‘The river heard us too.’ Udero Lal did not stay on to own the village. The light went out when the danger had passed, like someone who does the work and claims nothing. The children understood that the bridge was not made of stone. It was made of respect. That afternoon, the Indus refused to belong to one faith only.",
    "The Sindhi child grows up hearing Udero Lal as you hear about a bridge. Without the saint, the crocodile is only teeth and the mountain leopard is only fear. With him, the animals of the tale have a rule, and so do the people. Water is older than the names of temples and mosques. So it has a claim on worship: whoever drinks from the Indus learns to share the bank. If you see an angry river, do not ask first which faith the water belongs to. Ask whether you treated well those in the same boat as you.",
  ],
  "pk:4": [
    "The Indus river crosses Pakistan like a vein. The old myths call it Sindhu, and from that name came Sindh and, further on, the very idea of India. Without this water, the desert would beat the fields. With it, cities, wheat and stories five thousand years old were born. Children see the river on the map and realise that the country is not only mountain and not only sea. It is the path of the water between the two. In Karachi, at the end, the estuary mixes the river with salt. There, the legend is drunk in the shape of a port.",
    "A very old story says that the waters were held back. A dragon, called Vritra, had lain down in the throat of the mountain and would not let the river pass. The fields turned to dust. The wells sank lower. People asked for a way to the water, not a war to tell at night. Then a strong god bored through the rock, as someone opens a stiff door. The Indus poured out in a clear race. It is not a tale of wounds. It is a tale of thirst that ends. The dragon gave way, and the wheat could return.",
    "In the north, beside the Karakoram, people tell another origin, closer to the peri. A mountain fairy wept to see the valley dry. Her tears ran down in threads and joined into small streams. Together they went to swell the great river. The two stories do not fight. The dragon and the peri are two names for the same gratitude. someone, or something, opened the way for the water. A child can keep both without lying. One explains the force. The other explains the tenderness. Both end in the same river bed, on the way to the sea.",
    "Geography, said simply, does not wipe out the myth. The Indus rises far away, in the snows of Tibet. It enters Pakistan, takes in other rivers, and only rests near Karachi. Anyone living in Lahore, in Islamabad or in Sindh drinks, in some way, that journey. A grandfather on the bank would say that water, even well explained, stays sacred without ceasing to be true. In the legend, the hole in the mountain is what allows bread. The peri’s tear is what remembers care. Neither of them asks that a single drop be wasted.",
    "So Pakistan, in this tale, is the river itself. Without the Indus, the desert advances and the fables dry up. With the Indus, there is ancient civilisation and there are boats. There are also children pointing at the blue vein on the map. If you go to Karachi, remember that the salt water of the estuary was once snow and a story’s tear. The dragon held. The god opened. The peri wept. The country grew on the bank. Gratitude, here, is no decoration. It is the right way to treat the river that runs through us.",
  ],
  "bd:0": [
    "In Bangladesh, and also across the border in Bengal, children grow up inside a book. It is called Thakurmar Jhuli, the grandmother’s sack. Dakshinaranjan Mitra Majumder collected these tales. There are princes, princesses of gold, rivers of milk and the rakkhosh, the ogre of the swamps. The smell of the story is mustard oil and monsoon rain. But the real sack is not made of paper. It is the grandmother opening the clasp of memory at night, when the house is quiet. Without that voice, the country’s archive would stay shut on a shelf.",
    "One tale from the sack begins like this. There were three prince brothers, and the youngest was not the strongest. He was the one who listened. He had a faithful sister, who told him the truth even when it hurt. He also had a magic horse that knew the shortcuts of the rain. Far away, a rakkhosh guarded a crossing of the river. He could swim like a living boat. He let nobody carry home what the people needed: the saved seed, the song, the path. The monsoon was coming, like a third brother who had not yet spoken.",
    "The eldest prince tried force and came back wet, without a path. The middle one tried hurry and got lost in the cane field. The youngest asked his sister for advice. She said: ‘Do not spend the horse on the first fright. Wait for the monsoon to begin, and use each thing in its hour.’ He kept her words. When the sky opened, the ogre came out of his hole to hunt the noise. The prince did not go to meet him chest first. He went round, as water goes round a stone, and let the horse mark the safe track.",
    "The rain swelled the river. The rakkhosh was used to ruling the current. He did not read the new flood and ended up in a branch of water with no way out. There was no wound to describe. There was a tired ogre, caught by his own pride. Meanwhile the prince opened the basket of seed and gave it back to the village. His sister waited on the bank, without saying ‘I told you so’. Faithfulness needs no vanity. The monsoon, the story’s third brother, had done the heaviest work without asking for a crown.",
    "The grandmother closes the sack when the story ends, but the house is different. The Bengali child comes to know the rakkhosh by name and the monsoon by character. Without Thakurmar Jhuli, Bangladesh would lose an archive that does not fit in a museum alone. With it, every house has a grandmother opening the clasp. In this land of rivers, the ogre can swim. So can the prince, if he listens to his sister. If you have little in your hand, do not spend it on the first fright. Wait for the right rain, and choose the order, like the youngest.",
  ],
  "bd:1": [
    "In the Sundarbans, in the south of Bangladesh, the mangrove is a kingdom of roots, mud and salt water. There lives the Bengal tiger. It swims between the channels as if the forest were also river. He is the lord of that place, not a drawing on a wall. Anyone going in to gather honey does not go as an owner. They go as a visitor. Then they hear about Dakshin Rai, the tiger lord. The story belongs to the people of the mangrove, Hindu and Muslim. It does not belong to the city that only sees the map.",
    "The rule is old and clear. You ask Bonbibi’s permission before cutting, gathering or going on. Without permission, the tiger collects the debt of anyone who treats the forest like an endless larder. With permission, the step slows down. The slow step sees what the fast step hides. Dakshin Rai is not a villain. He is the limit with stripes. Bonbibi is the voice that teaches the limit without shaming the poor person who needs honey. Both fit in the same prayer, because the mangrove does not ask what faith the hungry person has. It asks whether they know how to ask.",
    "A man called Karim made ready his basket. He wanted to set off in the dark, to get there first. His mother put her hand on his arm: ‘You ask permission, or you do not get honey.’ He stopped and said Bonbibi’s name quietly. Only then did he get into the boat. The channel was quiet. Later in the morning he saw a tiger moving between the leaves, crossing shallow water. He did not run. He did not shout. He stepped back once, as the rite had taught. The animal went its own way without springing. The fear stayed, but it stayed useful. It had opened his eyes in time.",
    "Karim came back without a full basket. He carried only a little honey, gathered after waiting. He also carried the certainty of having seen the lord of the forest and still being alive. His mother did not call him a coward. She said: ‘Honey that comes with permission tastes better in the mouth.’ In the village, Hindus and Muslims repeat the same story of Bonbibi, each with their own words. The danger is the same. The forest is the same. That morning, the tiger was a judge without needing to punish. The delay of the rite saved the step.",
    "Without Bonbibi, the mangrove would be only a resource, wood and honey to be taken quickly. With her, it is a kingdom. Bangladesh learns to recognise the strength of the salt-water tiger. It does not do so with blind fear, but with respect for his home. The child of Khulna hears the protector before hearing the zoologist, and the two voices can get on well. The zoologist explains the animal. The legend explains the respect. If the honey comes, it comes with permission. If it does not, the life that comes back in the boat is already the greater prize.",
  ],
  "bd:2": [
    "The Padma is the great river of Bangladesh, the Ganges as it enters this land of delta. It is wide enough to eat a village in the flood and generous enough to give fish the next day. People do not speak of it the way they speak of a canal. They speak of a princess. Some say she is the goddess of the current. Others say she was a girl who turned into water to put out the thirst of a whole country. Every year, the flood is a national character. It climbs the steps, comes into the courtyards and forces the houses to learn how to float.",
    "The princess’s messenger is the hilsa, a silver fish they call the king. When it swims up the river, it seems like a message. The water is coming, and the net and the offering must be made ready. On the banks, families lay out flowers, rice or a small lamp. They say the princess’s name in the voice of someone talking to a powerful neighbour. It is not a payment to buy luck. It is courtesy. The Bengali child watches the level rise on a tree trunk. They repeat the name, so the flood has someone to talk to. Without a conversation, fear is left alone.",
    "There was a year when the Padma rose faster than the old people’s memory. One village mocked the offering and said a river does not listen to names. Another village, further down, offered its courtesy and tied up the roofs, the baskets and the beds, and waited. In the story, the princess is not a spell that wipes out the flood. She is the way to cross the flood without losing your head. Where there was mockery, the courtyard turned into a lake and the roof had to learn to float. Where there was respect, the current turned enough to leave a house standing.",
    "Nobody grew rich from the miracle, and the legend does not lie about the power of the river. The Padma keeps changing its banks, taking earth and giving fish back. The difference is that hardship, when it has a name, can be faced together. The boatmen followed the hilsa. The children helped carry the sacks up to the dry place. The princess, if she was ever a girl, chose to be a current to put out thirst. She did not choose to shame the poor. That is why the offering is small. A small thing, said in time, changes the way we look at the water.",
    "Without the princess, the flood would be only disaster. With her, Bangladesh lives an old conversation with the river. If you honour her, the roof has a better chance of staying put. If you offend her, the roof learns to float, and that lesson is cold. When the Padma rises, the child says the name. They do not laugh at anyone laying a flower in the mud. The hilsa goes past, silver, like a message that needs no letter. The country answers in the only way the delta knows: together, and facing the water.",
  ],
  "bd:3": [
    "In Bangladesh, a boat does not travel naked, and nor does a rickshaw in Dhaka. Tigers, palaces, film heroes, flowers and verses are painted on the planks and on the metal. The rain may be grey. The way there is not. This is folklore you see, not only folklore you hear. A gentle legend explains where so much colour came from. There was a poor painter, with stained hands, and a daughter who could not travel. Her legs tired early, and other people’s river went past her door without carrying her away. She sat at the window, counting boats that were not hers.",
    "The father had no money for a long journey. He had paints, brushes and a small boat borrowed for a week. He told his daughter: ‘If you cannot go to the world, the water can show it to you.’ He painted mountains, seas, cities, markets, animals and a sky with a thousand colours. He did not copy a single country. He copied the wish to see them all. The neighbours laughed on the first day and fell silent on the second. The boat looked like a page from the grandmother’s sack, only moving. The daughter watched from her seat, recognising stories in every figure.",
    "When the paint had dried, the father took the boat to the riverbank, slowly, so his daughter could go along. The moment the keel touched the water, the reflection opened like a road. The colours shook, but they did not fall apart. On the surface, the girl saw the countries going past, as if the boat were travelling without leaving that spot. It was no trick to fool a child. It was the whole gift: the world fits in a reflection, if someone had the patience to paint it. She did not have to be ashamed of her tired legs. The journey came to her.",
    "The neighbours asked the painter to paint the rickshaws in the street as well. He agreed, with the same rule as his daughter’s: no journey should be sad on the outside. So Dhaka filled with museums that pedal, and the monsoon boats went on carrying painted tigers over the real water. Later, the daughter told her nephews and nieces that she had seen many countries without leaving the bank. Some smiled. She did not mind. She knew the difference between an empty reflection and one made of work. Her father never grew rich. He grew necessary, which is another kind of fortune.",
    "A child climbing into a rickshaw in Dhaka is inside a tale, even if they are only going to school. Without the colours, the country of rain would be grey. With them, every journey is Thakurmar Jhuli moving in the street. The painter’s legend is, at heart, the truth of folk art. A poor person brought the world within reach of those who could not set off. The reflection, if it shows on the water, is a bonus. The main thing is that Bangladesh refuses naked transport. It paints the way. And the daughter at the window stops counting only other people’s boats.",
  ],
  "bd:4": [
    "The hills of Sylhet, in Bangladesh, smell of tea from early on. The history does not begin with magic. The British ordered the bushes planted. Assamese and Bengali workers then picked the leaf under a heavy sun, often far from home. That memory is hard, and it is not wiped out to make the story prettier. Beside it lives a story of the hills. Both fit in the same cup, if we are honest. One explains the empire. The other explains the affection with which this land calls its own the tea the world drinks.",
    "The legend says that a fairy of the hills saw a shepherd who was thirsty and had little shade. She gave him a small bush and only one rule. Tea, to be worth anything, is shared hot. The cup is not kept for your own mouth alone. The shepherd planted the bush beside the house and learned to dry the leaf. Then he called the neighbours when the water boiled. Nobody was left out. The fairy never came back asking for gold. In the story, she came back only to see whether the rule was kept. Where the cup went round, the hill seemed lower and the conversation longer.",
    "In time, the plantations grew, and the work of the pickers became bigger than the legend. Men and women, with baskets on their backs, made Sylhet a name that is recognised by its smell. The fairy does not replace those names. The child who drinks tea at home hears both layers. There is the charm of the bush offered and the tiredness of the person who picks it. If one is missing, the cup lies. The grandfather points at the leaf and says: ‘Without the pickers, there is nothing for the fairy to give.’ The sweetness of the tea should not hide the hand that brought it.",
    "Many people from Sylhet left for other countries, and took the tea in their luggage. In the diaspora, the hot cup calls up the hill even when the window looks onto another city. The fairy’s rule travels well: share, do not close the kitchen door. A new neighbour, who has never seen Bangladesh, learns the name of Sylhet from the steam. That way folklore does not stay stuck on the map. It travels. But as it travels, it does not forget where the leaf came from, or who paid for it in work. The empire planted. The people picked. The Bengali house made a rite of the gesture.",
    "The child of Sylhet drinks the origin slowly, with no hurry to choose a single beginning. The fairy gave the charm and the rule. The pickers gave the leaf. The British gave a chapter that is told in full, without false sweetness. In this cup, Bangladesh mixes empire and charm. It does not apologise for the mixture, as long as nobody is erased. What stays is the heat: tea, to be worth anything, is shared. If you fill a cup only for yourself, the legend is incomplete. Hold it out. Even far away, the hill recognises the steam of someone who does not drink alone.",
  ],
  "is:0": [
    "In Iceland, winter is a long night. For that darkness, the people invented thirteen small trolls, the jólasveinar, children of an old figure called Grýla. On the thirteen nights before Christmas they come down from the mountains, one by one. The gentle version does not put them in a sack of fear. They are like thirteen rascally uncles, each with just one fault. Their mother stays far away, at the back of the story. She is enough for a mild shiver, with no detail of terror.",
    "Each night has a name and a bad habit. One peeks into the cowshed. Another licks the spoons left in the pot. Another bangs on the doors. Another steals sausages from the pantry if nobody is watching. They are neither heroes nor monsters. They are visitors with bad habits and an icy laugh. Icelandic children count them on their fingers, from the first to the thirteenth. Without them, winter would be only night. With them, the darkness is broken into pieces that can be named.",
    "The stage is a shoe placed on the window ledge. If the child behaved well, in the morning there is a small present inside the shoe. If they were naughty, a potato may appear. It is not a cruel punishment. It is a message with a peel. In Reykjavik, a girl called Birta counted the nights on the kitchen calendar. On the night of the sausage thief she had hidden one in her pocket, just for fun. In the morning the shoe held no ribbon. It held a cold potato.",
    "Birta went red and put the sausage back in the pantry. She did not bang the door for the rest of the day. The next night the shoe held a wrapped biscuit. This time the rascally uncle seemed to have noticed the change. In old stories, the Christmas cat walks near Grýla. Here it stayed only as a distant warning. In the cold, a new piece of clothing warms you and brings cheer. Nobody described anything more than that. The laughter stayed with the children, near the window.",
    "In this way Iceland made a whole family for Christmas, with an icy sense of humour and clear rules. The shoe is the stage, the potato is the message, and each night has an owner. Grýla does not come into the children’s room. The thirteen come in, one at a time, and are gone before the short sun. If you put a shoe on the window ledge in this tale, remember the deal. Each uncle’s fault teaches without shouting. Winter, broken into thirteen names, is easier to cross.",
  ],
  "is:1": [
    "In the stones of Iceland lives the hidden people, the huldufólk. They are not stage fairies with wands and glitter. They are basalt neighbours. They live in boulders, in lava hills and in rocks that to a stranger look like plain geology. Some roads bend a little so as not to break a rock that is lived in. The legend is taken seriously enough to change maps. Tarmac that makes a curve for no other reason is already folklore written in engineering.",
    "The older people say that anyone throwing stones at random pays in small troubles. A tool breaks. A shortcut becomes confusing. Anyone who asks permission of a strange rock carries on in peace. No long speech is needed. You only stop, speak quietly and do not treat the rock like rubbish. The land of Iceland still smokes, shakes and moves. On ground like that, having invisible neighbours is a form of respect, almost tectonic, for what cannot be seen.",
    "Near a boulder at the edge of a building site, a boy called Einar was walking past with his friends. One of them picked up a loose stone to throw at the big block, just to hear it crack. Einar remembered his grandmother and held his wrist. ‘Ask permission first,’ he said. The others laughed, but they lowered their hands. Einar put his palm on the cold rock and said: ‘We are passing. We are not going to break your house.’ The wind stayed the same. Nobody came out of an invisible door. Even so, the gesture was made.",
    "Years before, in that same place, a road plan had moved a few metres to the side. The workers said the machine kept failing beside that boulder, and that it was more sensible to go round. The bend is still there. Drivers do not always know why. The children of the area do. They point at the basalt and say that hidden people live there. Those people live their own lives without asking for an audience. With that sentence, the lava stops being only dark rock. It gains a door.",
    "Without the huldufólk, Iceland would be a book of volcanoes and numbers. With them, every strange rock deserves a second of courtesy. If you find a boulder in the middle of a lava field, do not throw anything at it. Ask for passage. The ground of this island moves, and treating stones as houses is an old way of not ruling over everything. The tarmac that bends is not a mistake on the map. It is the legend managing, now and then, to be heard by engineers.",
  ],
  "is:2": [
    "In the south of Iceland, a jet of hot water gave its name to every geyser in the world. It is called Geysir. The word left the island and went off to learn other languages without losing its birthplace. Beside it, Strokkur spits every few minutes, as punctual as a fire clock. The ground there boils underneath. The steam rises, silica paints the edges, and anyone arriving too quickly can be burnt. The ground in this corner is not still, and not polite to those who push it.",
    "The legend does not choose a single author for the jet. Some say it is a troll down below, angry, spitting boiling water when someone treads on his roof. Others speak of a saint, or of the earth itself, tired of being punctured without respect. The science panel explains the steam and the pressure. The story by the fireside explains the rest: there is someone, or something, that answers back. The two versions live together in the same field. Nobody is forced to erase one so that the other can fit.",
    "A child called Sigrún went with her family to see Strokkur. She wanted to reach the edge and touch the water at the very moment it rose. Her father held her back behind the rope. ‘The clock belongs to the earth, not to you,’ he said. They waited. The ground made a round sound, like a covered pot. Then the column of water rose, white and tall, and fell in a warm rain that did not reach them. Sigrún laughed, with her hands wet only from steam. She had learned the timing without putting her finger in the fire.",
    "The Great Geysir, older and more irregular, sometimes sleeps for years and sometimes wakes with the roar of a fountain. People have got used to not giving it orders. Whoever waits, sees. Whoever forces the mouth of the earth, takes a risk. In this way Iceland named liquid fire for the whole planet. Without this name, the phenomenon would have a colder word. With it, every geyser in the world carries a little Icelandic. It does so even on another map, far from the snow and the moss.",
    "Watching the jet, the child still hears the troll of the legend and reads the panel on the same walk. Both lessons fit into a minute: the ground is not still, and hurry burns. If you go to the south of Iceland, stay behind the rope and count Strokkur’s minutes. The show does not need you to hurry it. The earth sets the hour. Respect, here, is as simple as not climbing over the rope. The steam rises all the same, punctual, and comes back down with no owner.",
  ],
  "is:3": [
    "The trolls of Iceland, like those of Norway, cannot bear the sun. They work at night, heavy and stubborn, and at the first light of morning their skin turns to basalt. On this island of long nights, the sun is two things at once. It is salvation for those who fear the darkness. It is also the end for those who use the night to do harm. In winter, when the light barely arrives, the trolls would have more hours. In summer, hardly any time is left to them. Their clock is the colour of the sky.",
    "In Vík, on the south coast, three stone needles rise out of the sea. They are called Reynisdrangar. The legend says they were trolls dragging a boat to land, or chasing a church. They did not want to see that church standing. They worked all night, hands on the keel and feet in the surf. They thought the darkness would last long enough. They did not look back, to the east, where the sky was changing from black to a strip of orange.",
    "The sun touched their backs before the boat reached the sand. There was no struggle and no long cry. There was stone. The three stayed where they were, tall, wet with spray, with no fingers left to pull anything. In the story, the boat got away. The church, if that was the target, stayed where it was. The children of Vík look at the needles and say ‘trolls’ without needing a guide. Erosion explains the shape. The legend explains the hurry. Both can sit on the same black beach.",
    "Basalt is the price of being late. Anyone doing wrong at night has to finish before the orange colour comes. Even then, the story does not advise starting. It advises remembering that the light arrives. In folk tales, many rocks on the island are people of stone caught halfway through a step. They do not move. They do not apologise. They stay there for the boats to see from far away and steer around. The sea beats, the years pass, and the shape still looks like a body that forgot the time.",
    "Without this race against the light, the needles would be only pretty rock. With it, the coast has drama and a warning. Iceland measures many things in hours of sun. If you see Reynisdrangar at daybreak, notice how the orange colour reaches the top first. The evil that trusted in the night loses its hands. In this story, the good is not a sword. It is the day, punctual, turning stubbornness into landscape. The basalt stays. So does the lesson.",
  ],
  "is:4": [
    "In the bay of Reykjavik opens the great gulf of Faxaflói. The ancients feared a creature of stories there, one that sinks boats when the sea is angry. They called it by various names, and one of them was skipaskeaðir, the creature that breaks hulls. A fin on the horizon was no pleasure trip. It was a warning. The fishermen lowered their voices and taught the children to respect the deep water. That old fear was not only fantasy. It was a way of not treating the sea as a back garden.",
    "For a long time Iceland also lived from whaling. The story had a harpoon, hard work and little conversation with the animal. The fin was danger or it was resource, almost never a neighbour. Children heard the two things mixed together: the monster that overturned boats and the enormous creature that men hunted. What was missing in between was a chapter. In it, the city knew how to keep still and let it pass. Folklore, when it grows well, also changes jobs.",
    "Today, in summer, whales come back to the bay like someone returning to a familiar street. Boats leave Reykjavik with people looking, not hunting. A girl called Birta went on one of those boats with her grandfather, who still knew the stories of fear. When the fin cut the water, the grandfather took off his hat, in the old way. ‘She is the lady of the bay,’ he said. ‘If the city is wise, it lets her pass.’ Nobody shouted a battle name. The boat kept its distance.",
    "Birta saw the dark back, the breath, and the slow disappearance. There was no film scare and no harpoon. There was size. On the same trip, the grandfather told the old chapter and the new one. There is the respect that came from fear. There is also the respect that comes from knowing that life is not there to decorate a trophy. Without the old legend, the care would be missing. Without the new one, the change would be missing. With both, the whale of the bay is a complete story, still being written.",
    "In this gulf, Iceland learned to look instead of only harpooning. That does not wipe out the past, and it does not celebrate it either. It sets another gesture beside it. If you see a fin in Faxaflói, do not turn it straight away into a monster or a toy. Remember the lady of the bay. The sea is still bigger than the city. Here, folklore does its best job: it teaches a fair distance. The fin on the horizon is the chapter that has not yet closed.",
  ],
  "tl:0": [
    "In the Tetum myth, the best loved in Timor-Leste, a boy called Bere found a wounded crocodile on the bank. Its leg was caught in a piece of old net. The animal could not get back to the deep water. Bere did not run away. He cut the net carefully, brought small fish, and stayed close until the leg stopped hurting. He spoke quietly, as if speaking to a tired grandfather. The crocodile listened and did not show its teeth.",
    "When the crocodile was strong again, it wanted to repay the kindness. It told Bere to climb on its back. Then it carried him across the sea, day after day, far from the dangers of the coast. The journey was slow and safe. The open sea did not frighten him. Bere held on to the scales and watched the sky change. There was no hurry. There was gratitude, which is a different wind. The kind boy had given care. The water animal replied with a road.",
    "Halfway across the sea, the crocodile’s body began to grow. Its back became land, with hills, rivers and a long coast. Bere climbed down and stepped onto new ground, still warm from the sun. He understood that he was no longer on top of a swimming animal. He was on top of an island. The crocodile had given a home. Without that boy, there would be no place to put your feet. The kindness had turned into a map.",
    "That is why the people of Timor call the crocodile grandfather. They do no harm to it for a joke or for pride. On the map, the island really has the outline of a crocodile lying in the sea. Children hear the grandfather in the water, when the waves beat like slow breathing. The sea around them reminds them of the journey. The land reminds them of the body. The people remember the good debt, still paid with respect.",
    "The legend asks for one word only, said seriously: family. Without that word, the crocodile would be only teeth. With it, the whole of Timor is a kindness that goes on. In the story, Bere did not ask for a kingdom. He asked for the leg to heal. He received an island, and the duty to treat it as you treat a grandfather. If you see the outline in the water, do not call it a nameless beast. Call it home, and treat it as family.",
  ],
  "tl:1": [
    "Mount Ramelau is the highest peak in Timor-Leste, and the ancient kingdoms knew its sacred name, Tatamailau. The legend says the dead sit on the summit, and that the mist is the smoke of their conversations. It is not an empty roof. It is the home of ancestors. When the country became independent, it looked to this mountain as an axis. It is a high place where the land recognises itself. Climbing up there asks for more than strong legs.",
    "The climb asks for a rite. You carry a pair of stones, you say a request, and you do not go up to boast. The girl Lita wanted to arrive first, just to tell the school she had beaten the mountain. Halfway up, the mist closed over the path, as if someone had wiped the line of the ground away. Lita stopped. She remembered her grandmother’s words: whoever climbs to boast, the mist eats the way. Not the person. The direction.",
    "Lita set down two stones on a flat rock. She lowered her voice and asked the grandparents of the summit for permission. She did not ask for a prize. She asked to visit. The mist did not all disappear, but it opened a crack, just enough for her feet to find solid ground. She climbed slowly, like someone entering a house where people are already talking. Each step was a greeting. The peak stopped being a trophy and became a visit.",
    "At the top, when the mist allowed it, Lita saw the sea on both sides of the island. She understood why so many had looked on Ramelau as an axis on the great days of the country. The dead, respected, let you see. If they are offended, there is only mist, and a person comes down without the image they came for. Lita did not shout her own name. She said the sacred name, Tatamailau, and thanked the view like someone thanking you for a place at the table.",
    "The Timorese child sees Ramelau and hears the grandparents before hearing the height in metres. Without the rite, the climb is tourism. With the rite, it is kinship. The legend asks what other sacred mountains ask, in another language: height is not kept in your pocket. If you go to the summit, take a pair of stones and a humble request. The dead talk in the smoke. You are going to visit them, not to beat them.",
  ],
  "tl:2": [
    "In the myth from Timor, the sun brother and the moon sister argued about who shone the most. The sun said his work was hot and great. The moon said her light arrived without burning the eyes. Neither of them wanted to give way. In Timorese thought, the pair is law: feto and mane, the female side and the male side, always together. A sky with no agreement is a world that limps.",
    "The argument turned into a race. One fled, the other chased, and the sky did not know what time it was. People could not get the fishing or the sowing right. The day came in the middle of the night, and the night in the middle of the day. The children were afraid when the darkness fell without warning. The old people said that a quarrel between siblings, if it does not end, robs the calendar from the people. The whole island waited for an agreement.",
    "Then came another way of telling the same myth, and the two fit together. The moon, gentler, agreed to shine for those who fear the dark, with a light that does not hurt. The sun, proud but necessary, kept the hot work of the day, which dries the maize and shows the paths. It was not a defeat. It was a sharing. Each one kept the shine they knew best how to make.",
    "They stopped chasing each other all the time. Day came to close the night, and night to close the day. They were like two hands taking turns over the same basket. The people knew again when to cast the net and when to rest. The quarrel did not vanish altogether. The siblings still look at each other from far away, one rising and the other setting. But the quarrel, with an agreement, became a calendar, and not confusion.",
    "The child hears the two in the sky and learns about complementing. Without the pair, the world limped. With the pair, the island has its hours. The pride of the sun and the calm of the moon do not wipe each other out. They complete each other. Nobody shines well alone, neither a person nor a star. If the argument reaches no agreement, the people are left without a clock. If it does, day and night can both work.",
  ],
  "tl:3": [
    "Tais is the cloth woven in Timor-Leste, and it is a treasure you wear. Every old kingdom, every family, has its own pattern, with diamonds that are not copied in a hurry. The legend of its origin tells of two doors. In one, a woman dreamed the first motif and woke up to weave it. In the other, the crocodile grandfather gave the colours of his own skin for the thread. Both honour the hands. Without hands, the dream does not become cloth.",
    "The weaver Domingas worked for hours and days, and the pattern came to life slowly on the loom. Her daughter Bia sat beside her and watched the diamond close up, colour by colour. Her mother said that each figure is a message: of birth, of alliance, of a farewell. Without tais, a rite is incomplete, because the cloth that names the occasion is missing. The time of the loom cannot be hurried. The thread decides the pace, and it almost never has any.",
    "Bia asked which of the two origins was the real one, the dream or the crocodile. Domingas answered that both fit, if the hands are honoured. The dream gave the design. The grandfather of the sea gave the colours. The rest was women’s work, from early morning until the light dropped. Bia put her fingers on the thread without pulling. She learned that a country can also be seen. If nobody weaves, the country becomes invisible, even if the map exists.",
    "There were hard years, when many things were broken and many names almost wiped out. Tais, older than the flag, went on saying those names in its patterns. A grandmother wrapped a child in the family cloth, and the child became the legend walking about. It was not only warmth. It was an open letter that the war could not quite tear up. The loom, in the shade of the house, was a memory that did not fit in a pocket.",
    "The legend asks you to weave, and the thread decides the rest. Wrapped in a tais, the Timorese child carries the dream, the crocodile and the weaver’s hours on their shoulders. Each diamond is a message that is not shouted. It is read up close, with respect, as you read a letter from the family. If you see a tais in a market or at a festival, do not call it just fabric. Ask about the pattern. There is the name that the hands wanted to keep.",
  ],
  "tl:4": [
    "Ataúro, the island north of Dili, has dry hills and very rich water. The reefs are still whole, and that is why the island is a sanctuary for corals. The legend speaks of a fish of many colours that talks to honest fishermen. It does not shout. It speaks quietly, like someone in a meeting. To anyone using a bomb in the sea, the fish says nothing. The treasure here is not sold in ice, if you want it to come back the next day.",
    "The fisherman Leo went out in the morning with a simple hook and his daughter Ana in the small bow. The fish came close to the rocks. It spoke to those who knew how to listen. ‘Take what the pot asks for, and leave the rest to grow.’ Leo caught little and came back. Ana realised that the reef was not a postcard to look at and forget. It was a gathering of lives. The contrast of the island fitted that sentence: dry land above, rich water below, and a rule in between.",
    "A man from outside wanted a shortcut and blew up the water. The bang passed, the fish stopped speaking, and a branch of coral broke in the silence. No party and no full basket was worth that. Ana was angry and sad at the same time. Her father said the bomb is the crime in this story, and that an honest hook is a conversation. Whoever deafens the sea is left with no answer, and still calls that luck.",
    "Leo and Ana went to the calm water, with no extra nets and no hurry for ice. They said sorry out loud, not to the man, but to the reef. The next day the fish spoke again, short and clear, and let the pot fill up once more. The meeting had reopened. Ana dipped only her eyes over the edge of the boat. She saw the colours moving like people in a square. The broken coral needed time. Respect gave it that time.",
    "The child of Ataúro dives into this legend while there is still time. Without the fish that speaks, the reef is just a pretty picture. With it, the reef is a shared home. If the fish is heard, it comes back tomorrow. If it is deafened, the legend ends, and the coral ends with it. On this island, Timor-Leste teaches the sea that is still whole. The honest hook asks. The bomb does not ask. The answer, if it comes, comes in colour and in a good silence.",
  ],
  "af:0": [
    "Laila arrived at the lakes of Band-e Amir with her dark hair in the wind and her embroidered dress shining. The little coins on the dress made a tiny sound at every step. The water was a vivid blue, so clean that it looked like new paint. The adults stopped on the agreed bank. She stopped too, even though she wanted to go further.",
    "She did not throw stones. She did not go into the water beyond the place where the adults had their feet. The bank was the limit, and the blue did not need a game. A cousin crouched to wet his hand, and Laila did the same, without going further. The fish moved like clear shadows. Nobody shouted to frighten them.",
    "Laila left her hand on the edge, only up to the wrist, and watched. The fish came near and went away again, in no hurry to be caught. She did not make a net with her fingers. She did not take one away in a jar. The lake was not a box. It was a blue house, and the visit had to be short and quiet.",
    "Before they went back, her mother looked at the ground around the simple picnic. There was no forgotten bottle, no bag, no fruit peel. Laila took a short turn and picked up a piece of paper that the wind had brought. She put it in the basket. The lakes stayed blue and free of rubbish, just as they had found them.",
    "If you go to a very blue lake, do not throw stones at it. Do not go further than the adults. Wet your hand at the bank, look at the fish and let them stay. Take no bottle, no paper, and no jar with a fish inside. The colour is prettier when the ground is clean. You have taken the blue home in your memory, and that is enough.",
  ],
  "af:1": [
    "Above the hills of Kabul, the sky was wide and clear. Laila and her neighbour Sami had two paper kites, one blue and one the colour of sand. The strings were smooth, with no glass and no knots made for cutting. Laila’s embroidered dress, with its little coins, moved in the wind. The two of them stood side by side, not one against the other.",
    "The kites went up together, as if they had agreed the height. When one came down, the other waited, and nobody pulled to bring the other down. Sami laughed when his string made a curve. Laila laughed too, without turning the curve into a contest. The wind belonged to everyone. There was no prize for cutting off your neighbour’s piece of sky.",
    "The strings touched up there and made a small knot. Instead of pulling hard, they brought the kites down slowly, both at the same time. They sat on the earth and undid the knot with their fingers, talking quietly. ‘Slowly,’ said Laila. ‘If we pull, it will break.’ Sami nodded and held the paper so it would not crumple.",
    "The knot came out. The kites were whole, without a tear and without a winner. They let them go again for a moment, just to see them side by side once more. Then they called them in calmly, and each one came down to the hand that had brought it. Laila pressed hers against her dress. Sami did the same with his. The two smiles fitted on the same hill.",
    "If you fly a paper kite, use a smooth string, with no glass. Fly beside the others, not over them to cut them down. If the strings touch, come down and undo the knot calmly. Both kites can come back to your hands whole. You do not need to bring down anyone else’s sky to make yours higher.",
  ],
  "af:2": [
    "At home, in Kabul, there was a ripe pomegranate on the table. Laila put a bowl underneath and opened the fruit with her hands, slowly, without squashing it. The seeds fell out red and whole, like beads. There was no angry blow, and no juice jumping onto the wall. The pomegranate opened because it was ready, not because someone wanted to break it.",
    "Three children were waiting, with washed hands. Laila shared the seeds into four little piles, one for each of them and one for herself. Nobody grabbed the whole bowl. The youngest tasted first and closed her eyes, happy. The taste was sweet and a little sharp. It was enough for everyone without a second pomegranate.",
    "They ate slowly, counting the seeds for fun and not from greed. If one little pile looked smaller, Laila passed two seeds over from her own share. The coins on her dress knocked against each other when she leaned over. Nobody left the table angry. The bowl emptied in a fair way, and the juice stayed on their fingers, not on the floor.",
    "The skins, still pretty, did not go into the street. Laila took them to the garden, to the corner where the earth likes fruit scraps. She covered them with a little soil, without making an ugly heap. ‘Here they still serve a purpose,’ said her mother. The street stayed clean. The finished pomegranate had not become rubbish at other people’s doors.",
    "If you open a pomegranate, do it over a bowl and do not squash it. Share the seeds with everyone at the table, even if your hand wants the whole pile. The skins go to the garden, not into the street. A fruit goes a long way when it is shared. You are left with sweet fingers and a peaceful table.",
  ],
  "af:3": [
    "In the morning, in Kabul, her mother made tea and warm bread from the oven. It was not a celebration. It was the custom of taking a little something to the door when the neighbour passed by. Laila picked up the tray with both hands, as she had been taught. Her embroidered dress swung, and the little coins kept time with her steps. Her dark hair was tied back so it would not fall in the tea.",
    "The neighbour was waiting on the step, with a smile like any other day. Laila offered the bread and the cup without apologising and without making a speech. ‘Good morning,’ she said. ‘It is hot.’ The neighbour thanked her and drank there, standing up, like someone receiving something ordinary and good. Nobody was sent away. The door did not shut in anybody’s face.",
    "The neighbour’s son came too, still rubbing the sleep from his eyes. There was enough bread, and Laila cut one more piece without doing big sums. The tray stayed steady in her hands, level, without a wobble of hurry. The tea reached a second small cup. The morning went on simply, and the simplicity was the gift.",
    "When the tray came back to the kitchen, it still had crumbs and a little tea at the bottom. Laila did not throw the crumbs into the street. She shook them onto a plate for the birds in the courtyard and washed the cup. Her mother nodded. ‘That is how you treat those who pass by,’ she said. Laila dried her hands. The custom fitted into one morning and needed no audience.",
    "If you take bread and tea to someone, use both hands and do not shut the door. Do not turn kindness into a story of pity, or into an enormous favour. A hot mouthful, given as something normal, is enough. If one more person comes, cut another piece. You grow bigger when the door stays open.",
  ],
  "af:4": [
    "In the workshop in Herat, the looms held carpets with geometric figures, only diamonds, only lines, no letters. Laila went in slowly, so as not to raise dust onto the wool. The embroidered dress looked like a cousin of those designs, also full of colour. A weaver was working a blue corner. In the middle of the row there was a loose woollen thread, asking to be pulled.",
    "Laila almost pulled it, because a loose thread looks like the start of a game. She stopped in time. If she pulled, the whole row might come undone, and the design would stop being a diamond. She called the weaver in a low voice. ‘There is a thread here,’ she said. She did not touch it again. Her hands stayed together in front of her dress.",
    "The weaver smiled and put the thread back in place, with a small hook and a lot of calm. The diamond closed up again. There were no letters in the design, only the figure that was already there. ‘Thank you for not pulling,’ she said. Laila felt her cheeks warm, in a good way. The carpet still belonged to the workshop, not to a trophy.",
    "On the floor there were woollen scraps, short and colourful. Laila asked for a broom and swept them into a basket, for the next weaving. She did not fill her pockets. She did not ask for a corner of the carpet to take away. The weaver pointed at the basket. ‘That will still be a flower in another design,’ she said. Laila left the basket beside the loom and went out with empty hands.",
    "If you see a loose thread in a carpet, do not pull it to see what happens. Call the person who weaves and let them put it back. You can sweep the scraps into a basket, if you are allowed, and do not take the carpet. The figures stay in the workshop. You take away only the care of not undoing what someone else has made.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
